  "content": "# Travel Journal: The Ascent to Kharbu\n\n*October 14th*\n\nThe road ended where the map did, at a weathered wooden sign half-swallowed by moss, and from there the mountain claimed the rest of my journey. I left my pack at the edge of the trail and began the climb on foot, my boots finding the familiar rhythm of scree and root that every fieldwork season teaches you to trust. The morning air was thin and sharp, carrying the mineral scent of wet stone and something greener beneath it\u2014alpine meadows still holding the last of their summer. Above me, the peak wore a fresh cap of snow even though it had only begun to frost the night before, and I noted the cloud line sitting unusually low, a grey bank drifting through the mid-slopes that I did not care for. I have learned to respect the mountains' moods, and this one felt watchful.\n\nBy midday the weather had turned against me in the way it always does up here, without ceremony. A wind built in the valley and came rushing up the slope, carrying fine snow that stung my cheeks and blurred the trail into a wash of white. I pulled my hood low and pressed on toward the village",
